Partial template specialization provides a specialized version of a class template for a *subset* of its template parameters, leaving some generic. It's like an 'overload' for class templates.
```cpp
template<typename T>
class MyClass<T, int> {
    // Specialized implementation for U = int
};
```

Function templates use *overload resolution* instead of partial specialization. If you want a function to behave differently for specific types or patterns, you simply provide overloaded function templates (or non-template functions) that are more specialized. The compiler's overload resolution rules will then select the best match.
```cpp
// Primary template
template<typename T> void func(T val) {}
// "Partial specialization" via overloading
template<typename T> void func(T* val) {}
// Full specialization via overloading
void func(int* val) {}
```

```cpp
template<typename T>
class Wrapper {
public:
    void print() { std::cout << "Generic Wrapper" << std::endl; }
};

template<typename T>
class Wrapper<T*> { // Partial specialization for any pointer type
public:
    void print() { std::cout << "Pointer Wrapper" << std::endl; }
};
// Usage:
// Wrapper<int> w1; w1.print(); // Generic
// Wrapper<int*> w2; w2.print(); // Pointer
```

```cpp
template<typename T, int N>
class Array {
    // T data[N]; // This would fail for N=0
public:
    void printSize() { std::cout << "Array size: " << N << std::endl; }
};

template<typename T>
class Array<T, 0> { // Partial specialization for N = 0
public:
    void printSize() { std::cout << "Empty Array (size 0)" << std::endl; }
};
// Usage:
// Array<int, 5> a1; a1.printSize(); // Array size: 5
// Array<double, 0> a2; a2.printSize(); // Empty Array (size 0)
